; Written by: 	Magnus Olsson of Sweden.
; Obtained from: 	The 1998 SSEM Programming Competition.
; Description: 	"uses the CRT as an X-Y display to plot a parabola by setting bits in the unused upper half of lines 1-22.
;               The parabola is computed as the solution of a difference equation."
; Parameter Lines: 	-
; Result Lines: 	(see description)

01 LDN 29    ; ACC = -line29.
02 SUB 29    ; ACC = line29 - line29 = 0.
03 STO 29    ; Clear line29; this is the loop termination counter/state.
04 CMP       ; If ACC is negative, skip the halt. Here ACC is 0, so no skip.
05 STP       ; Halt once the termination condition has been reached.
06 LDN 29    ; ACC = -line29.
07 STO 29    ; Store that updated state back to line29.
08 LDN 22    ; ACC = -line22.
09 SUB 29    ; ACC = line22 - line29.
10 STO 30    ; Save the new Y/display value in scratch line30.
11 LDN 30    ; ACC = -line30.
12 STO 22    ; Update CRT/display line22 with the new plotted value.
13 LDN 8     ; ACC = -line8.
14 SUB 28    ; ACC = line8 - line28.
15 STO 30    ; Save the updated difference-equation term in line30.
16 LDN 30    ; ACC = -line30.
17 STO 8     ; Rewrite line8 with the new coefficient/state.
18 SUB 27    ; ACC = line30 - line27.
19 STO 12    ; Rewrite line12, making the plotting code self-modify for the next point.
20 LDN 28    ; ACC = -line28.
21 SUB 26    ; ACC = line28 - 1.
22 STO 28    ; Update the stepping value in line28.
23 LDN 28    ; ACC = -line28.
24 STO 28    ; Negate/store it back, completing the state update for the next loop.
25 JMP 31    ; Branch via line31 back to the start of the loop.
26 NUM 1     ; Constant 1.
27 NUM -8192 ; Constant used to shift the self-modified display update.
28 NUM -6    ; Difference-equation step value.
29 NUM 131072; Loop/state word and initial value used by the termination test.
30 NUM 0     ; Scratch line for intermediate values.
31 NUM 0     ; Encoded jump target used by line25 to restart execution at line01.
